## Macros &amp; Functions
<hr style="width:100%;border-bottom:3px solid black;">

Targets have a complete, auto-generated [API reference]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/api).  But `compose.mk` also ships a large collection of GNU-make **macros / functions** that are **not** part of the reflected API reference.  This page collects that side of the library.

!!! tip "Targets vs. Macros, in one line"
    A **target** is a rule you *run* (`./compose.mk io.bash`, or `${make} io.bash`
    recursively).  A **macro** is text you *expand* in place: `${name}` (nullary)
    or `$(call name, arg1, arg2)` (parametric).  Many things exist as **both**.

### Why macros exist
<hr style="width:95%;border-bottom:1px dashed black;">

As covered in [Style & Idioms]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/style), the project *prefers* targets (they compose and reflect better), but reaches for a macro in three situations:

* **Readability:** `${stream.stdin}` reads better than `cat /dev/stdin`, and `$(call log.target)` better than the equivalent `printf`/`cut` incantation.
* **Performance:** a macro expands in the current shell, avoiding the cost of a recursive `${make}` for hot, inner-loop helpers.
* **Scaffolding:** some macros emit *whole or partial makefiles* at parse-time (most the "import" macros).

### Calling conventions
<hr style="width:95%;border-bottom:1px dashed black;">

```Makefile {.language-makefile}
# Nullary expansion (no arguments). Just reference it:
demo:; cat input.json | ${stream.peek} | ${jq} .

# Parametric ones use $(call ..)
demo:; $(call log.target, starting up)

# Many targets ALSO publish a macro of the same name, 
# which saves a process and prevents recursive re-parse.

# Macro form
demo:; printf 'a\nb\n' | ${stream.nl.to.space}        

# Target form (equivalent)
demo:; printf 'a\nb\n' | ${make} stream.nl.to.space   
```

In [CMK-lang]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/compiler) the call syntax is sugared:
`cmk.fn(args)` compiles to `$(call fn, args)`, and `this.foo` compiles to
`${make} foo` (see the [Quick Reference]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/cmk-quick-ref)).

### Discovering &amp; reflecting on macros
<hr style="width:95%;border-bottom:1px dashed black;">

Two reflection helpers (each is *itself* available as both a target and a macro --
see [Reflection]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/reflection)) let you inspect any
macro or define at runtime:

```bash {.cli_example}
# Show the raw, unexpanded value of a macro/variable:
$ ./compose.mk mk.get/stream.nl.to.space
xargs

# Dump a `define ... endef` block verbatim
$ ./compose.mk mk.def.read/mk.unpack.kwargs
```

{{site.api_link('mk.get', mkdocs)}} expands a name; {{site.api_link('mk.def.read', mkdocs)}} reads a define block.  Both are the reflection primitives that the importer macros (`mk.import.def` / `mk.import.target`) build on.

